# ==== HEADER-COLON ANCHOR · the other containers (AXIS D)
#
# ⚠ INPUT→OUTPUT, not a fixpoint — see the `.expected` companion. `gg fmt`
# REJOINS a wrapped header onto one line, so the wrapped spelling exists only
# on pass one.
#
# The struct cell lives in `header_colon_anchor_multiline.gg`; these are its
# siblings in the one spelling that PARSES for each — the BRACKETED GENERIC
# parameter list. Two spellings that cannot be written are named here rather
# than silently omitted:
#   * a wrapped `extends` list (`trait T extends\n  A,\n  B:`) is a PARSE
#     ERROR — the list has no bracket to hold the line open;
#   * a wrapped `extern` header is likewise a parse error — `extern "C":` has
#     no bracket either.
#
# The EQUIP cell is GREEN AT HEAD for the ANCHOR question: its old anchor was
# the equipped TYPE's span end, which on this shape is accidentally on the
# header's last line already. It is kept as an axis cell and RED-verified by
# BREAK-AND-WATCH: anchor on the equip block's own `span.start` and the comment
# drops into the body.
#
# The equip line's SEPARATOR (`equip [T] Cell[T]:`, not `equip [T]Cell[T]:`) is
# not this file's subject — `equip_generic_params_space.gg` owns that axis.
#
# RED, observed on the pre-fix build: the enum and trait comments were emitted
# on their own line at body indent, as a leading comment of the first member.

enum Choice[T]:    # enum multi-line header trailing
    One(T)

trait Holder[T]:    # trait multi-line header trailing
    T get(self)

struct Cell[T]:
    T v

equip [T] Cell[T]:    # equip multi-line header trailing
    T get(self):
        return self.v

void main():
    print(1)
